<!--
    The copy mark: two rounded sheets, the back one peeking at the top right.

    ## Provenance — and a mistake worth keeping

    Supplied by the reader as `copy_line.svg` (an iconfont-style SVG), and the path below is that
    file's own `d` attribute **verbatim**. It is one path with three subpaths: the front sheet's
    outer contour, its inner contour (which is what makes the frame a frame), and the back sheet.

    The first version of this file did **not** copy it verbatim. It split the path into two
    elements and turned the third subpath's *relative* move into an absolute one by hand — and got
    it wrong, because `m170.666666 -256` is relative to where the *second* subpath ended
    (618.666667, 362.666667), not to where the first began. It wrote `M789.333333 21.333333`
    instead of `M789.333333 106.666667`: the back sheet sat **85.333 units** — exactly the inner
    contour's own offset — too high, so the two sheets were 256 apart instead of 170.667. The
    reader caught it by opening the source, which is what the report "复制图标画的有瑕疵" was.

    The lesson is `AGENTS.md`'s: a path is data, not prose. Copy it, and if a conversion cannot be
    avoided, convert it with arithmetic rather than by eye. Only two mechanical edits are made
    here, and neither moves a point: `fillColor` becomes opaque black so that `Icon`'s tint
    reaches it, and `fillType` becomes `evenOdd`, which is what turns the nested subpaths into
    holes whatever their winding direction.

    The equivalent figure in a licence-checked set is Google's Material Symbols `content_copy` in
    the **rounded**, *outlined* style (Apache-2.0).
-->
